    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Riesz representation — every continuous linear functional is $\\langle \\cdot, y \\rangle$\n\nFor Hilbert space $H$ and continuous linear $\\varphi : H \\to \\mathbb{F}$, there's a unique $y_\\varphi \\in H$ with $\\varphi(x) = \\langle x, y_\\varphi \\rangle$ for all $x$. The map $\\varphi \\leftrightarrow y_\\varphi$ is an isometric (anti-)isomorphism $H^* \\cong H$.\n\nThis is **the** distinguishing feature of Hilbert space — the dual is \"the same as\" the original. In Banach spaces ($L^p, \\ell^p$ for $p \\ne 2$), the dual is a *different* space ($L^q$ with $1/p + 1/q = 1$).\n\n## Adjoints of bounded operators\n\nFor bounded $T : H \\to H$, there's a unique bounded **adjoint** $T^*$ with $\\langle T x, y \\rangle = \\langle x, T^* y \\rangle$ for all $x, y$. In matrix notation on $\\mathbb{C}^n$, $T^* = T^H$ (conjugate transpose).\n\nA bounded operator is:\n\n- **Self-adjoint** if $T = T^*$. In matrices: Hermitian. Has real spectrum.\n- **Normal** if $T T^* = T^* T$. In finite dimensions — or for *compact* normal operators — this gives an orthonormal basis of eigenvectors. In general it does not: a bounded normal operator may have no eigenvectors at all. What survives is that $T$ is unitarily equivalent to a multiplication operator (notebook 7).\n- **Unitary** if $T T^* = T^* T = I$. Norm-preserving; matrix-version of \"rotation\".\n\nThese are exactly the structural features that make finite-dimensional linear algebra work, lifted to infinite dimensions.\n\n## Why Hilbert is special\n\nIn summary, Hilbert space recovers most of the Euclidean structure:\n- Orthogonality and projections.\n- Angles via $\\cos\\theta = \\langle x, y \\rangle / (\\|x\\|\\|y\\|)$.\n- Self-duality (Riesz).\n- Spectral decomposition for self-adjoint operators (notebook 7).\n\nWithout Hilbert structure (just Banach), we lose all of this. Many problems that look hopeless in $L^1$ or $L^\\infty$ are clean in $L^2$ — that's why physics and engineering live in $L^2$ when they can.\n\n## Tying back\n\nWe've built two pillars:\n\n- **Banach spaces** — completeness + norm.\n- **Hilbert spaces** — completeness + inner product, plus geometry.\n\nNext: **bounded linear operators** — the morphisms between these spaces — and their algebra.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Reference\n\nConway, J.B. (2007) *A Course in Functional Analysis*. 2nd edn. New York, NY: Springer New York. doi:[10.1007/978-1-4757-4383-8](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4757-4383-8) — ch. I, *Hilbert Spaces*.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
}